/* Nelson Textbook data chunk 03/07; generated only from absi/Nelson.zip. */
window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__=(window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__||[]).concat([{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-037-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"One of the MosT common causes of allergic contact dermatitis is","o":["neomycin","topical antihistamines","topical anesthetics","topical corticosteroids","ethylenediamine 533 [Continuation on source page 544]"],"k":0,"ex":"Neomycin in over-the-counter ointments is a very common cause of allergic contact dermatitis.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n37.(A). Neomycin sulfate is present in many nonprescription topical antibiotic\npreparations, and thus children are frequently exposed at an early age. It is one of the\nmost common causes of allergic contact dermatitis, and use of combination products of\nneomycin with other antibiotics, antifungals, or corticosteroids may induce co-\nreactivity with these chemically-unrelated substances.\n\nOptions:\nB–E are less common.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 543 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":37,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-039-3","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Seborrheic dermatitis is a chronic inflammatory disease most common in infancy and adolescence, it is characterized by the following ExCEPT","o":["manifest in 1st mo of life","self-resolves by 1 yr","cradle cap","pruritis","post inflammatory pigmentary changes"],"k":3,"ex":"Infantile seborrhoeic dermatitis: cradle cap, onset in first months, usually non-itchy, resolves in the first year.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n39.(D). A greasy, scaly, erythematous papular dermatitis, which is usually nonpruritic in\ninfants, may involve the face, neck, retroauricular areas, axillae, umbilicus, and diaper\narea.\n\nOptions:\nA–C,E are features; D is the exception.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 544 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":39,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-040-3","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Initial management for infantile seborrheic dermatitis is","o":["conservative","low-potency topical corticosteroids","topical antifungal","topical calcineurin inhibitors","oral antifungal agents"],"k":0,"ex":"Reassure; use emollients and gentle shampooing; steroids/antifungals only for severe cases.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n40.(A). Initial management for infantile seborrheic dermatitis is generally conservative\ngiven the self-limited nature of this condition. Emollients, baby oil, gentle shampooing\nwith non-medicated baby shampoo and gentle use of a soft brush to remove scales are\nusually effective measures.\n\nOptions:\nB–E are second step.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 544 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":40,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-043-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Guttate psoriasis can be confused with the following EXCEPT","o":["viral exanthems","secondary syphilis","nummular dermatitis","pityriasis rosea 534 [Continuation on source page 545]","pityriasis lichenoides chronica (PLC)"],"k":2,"ex":"Guttate psoriasis resembles viral exanthems, secondary syphilis, pityriasis rosea and PLC; nummular dermatitis is a plaque-type mimic.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n43.(C). The differential diagnosis of plaque type psoriasis includes nummular\ndermatitis, tinea corporis, seborrheic dermatitis, postinfectious arthritis syndromes,\nand pityriasis rubra pilaris.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,D,E are mimics; C is the exception.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 544 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":43,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-044-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"The treatment of psoriasis should be viewed as a 4-tier process. The second tier of therapy is","o":["phototherapy","high-potency corticosteroids","tar preparations","methotrexate","infliximab"],"k":0,"ex":"Psoriasis therapy tiers: topical, phototherapy, systemic (methotrexate), biologics.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n44.(A).\n\nOptions:\nB,C are first tier; D third; E fourth.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 545 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":44,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-045-3","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The generalized eruption of pityriasis rosea resembles a number of other diseases. Of the following, the MOsT important is","o":["secondary syphilis","drug eruptions","viral exanthems",".guttate psoriasis","nummular dermatitis"],"k":0,"ex":"Pityriasis rosea can mimic secondary syphilis, so syphilis is the most important differential.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n45.(A).\n549\n[Source page 560]\n\nOptions:\nB–E are lesser mimics.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 545 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":45,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-050-3","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A 12-year-old child presented with brawny edema of the face and neck that spreads rapidly to involve the thorax and arms. The face acquires a waxy, mask-like appearance. The involved areas feel indurated, woody, nonpitting, and are not sharply demarcated from normal skin. Of the following, the MOsT likely diagnosis is","o":["scleredema of buschke","scleroderma","morphea","dermatomyositis","subcutaneous fat necrosis"],"k":0,"ex":"Scleredema of Buschke: rapid brawny, woody, non-pitting oedema of face, neck, trunk after infection.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n50.(A).\n\nOptions:\nB,C,D,E have other features.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 546 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":50,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-052-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Cutis laxa is characterized by all the following ExCEPT","o":["widespread folds of lax skin","characteristic facial features","hypermobility of the joints","normal tensile strength of the skin","hoarse cry"],"k":2,"ex":"Cutis laxa has lax skin and a hoarse cry but no joint hypermobility (unlike Ehlers-Danlos).","more":"Nelson explanation:\n52.(C). Hyperelasticity and hypermobility of the joints are not present as they are in the\nEhlers-Danlos syndrome.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,D,E are features; C is the exception.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 546 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":52,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-053-3","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is characterized by the following EXCEPT","o":["post-term birth","skin hyperelasticity","easy bruising .severe joint hypermobility","subcutaneous spheroids 536 [Continuation on source page 547]"],"k":0,"ex":"Classic EDS: hyperelastic skin, easy bruising, joint hypermobility, premature birth (not post-term).","more":"Nelson explanation:\n53.(A). Premature birth caused by rupture of membranes.\n\nOptions:\nB,C,D,E are features.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 546 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":53,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-054-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Urticaria pigmentosa is the most common form of mastocytosis. It can be confused with the following EXCEPT","o":["drug eruptions",". herpes simplex","pigmented nevi","insect bites","bullous impetigo"],"k":1,"ex":"Urticaria pigmentosa mimics drug eruptions, pigmented naevi, insect bites; herpes simplex mimics solitary mastocytoma.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n54.(B). The differential diagnosis of solitary mastocytomas includes recurrent bullous\nimpetigo, herpes simplex, congenital melanocytic nevi, and juvenile xanthogranuloma.\nUrticaria pigmentosa can be confused with drug eruptions, postinflammatory\npigmentary change, juvenile xanthogranuloma, pigmented nevi, ephelides, xanthomas,\nchronic urticaria, insect bites, and bullous impetigo. Diffuse cutaneous mastocytoma\nmay be confused with epidermolytic hyperkeratosis.\n\nOptions:\nA,C,D,E are mimics.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 547 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":54,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-058-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Infiltrative or destructive disorders that may produce atrophy of sweat glands by pressure or scarring include the following ExCEPT","o":["scleroderma","acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans","burns","ichthyoses","Sjogren syndrome"],"k":3,"ex":"Sweat gland atrophy follows scarring/pressure disorders; ichthyoses cause obstruction, not atrophy.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n58.(D). Obstruction of sweat glands may occur in miliaria and in a number of\ninflammatory and hyperkeratotic disorders, such as the ichthyoses, psoriasis, lichen\nplanus, pemphigus, porokeratosis, atopic dermatitis, and seborrheic dermatitis.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,C,E cause atrophy.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 547 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":58,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-066-3","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Yellow nail syndrome manifests as thickened, excessively curved, slow-growing yellow nails without lunulae. Associated systemic diseases include the following ExCEPT","o":["bronchiectasis","recurrent bronchitis","chylothorax","chronic hepatitis","focal edema of the limbs and face"],"k":3,"ex":"Yellow nail syndrome is linked to lymphatic hypoplasia: bronchiectasis, chylothorax, limb oedema.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n66.(D). Deficient lymphatic drainage, caused by hypoplastic lymphatic vessels, is\nbelieved to lead to the manifestations of this syndrome.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,C,E are associated.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 549 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":66,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-069-3","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Nail changes may be particularly associated with various other diseases. The following matchings are true EXCEPT","o":["psoriasis : pitting, yellow-brown discoloration, and thickening","lichen planus : violaceous papules in the proximal nail bed","Darier disease: red or white streaks that extend longitudinally","alopecia areata: Transverse rows of fine pits","acrodermatitis enteropathica: subungual parakeratotic scaling and thickening 539 [Continuation on source page 550]"],"k":4,"ex":"Nail findings: psoriasis pitting, lichen planus, Darier streaks, alopecia areata pits; acrodermatitis enteropathica gives Beau lines.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n69.(E). Patients with acrodermatitis enteropathica may have transverse grooves (Beau\nlines) and nail dystrophy as a result of periungual dermatitis.\n\nOptions:\nA–D are correct matches.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 549 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":69,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-071-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Angular cheilitis is characterized by inflammation and fissuring at the corners of the mouth, often with associated erosion, maceration, and crusting. Of the following, the LEAsT likely cause is","o":["chapping",". chronic lip lickers","excessive salivation","nutritional deficiencies","contact dermatitis to toothpaste"],"k":3,"ex":"Common causes are chapping, lip licking, salivation and contact dermatitis; nutritional deficiency is a less frequent cause.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n71.(D). Nutritional deficiencies are a less-frequent etiology.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,C,E common.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"⚠ Key per Nelson; iron/B-vitamin deficiency is a recognised cause","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 550 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":71,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-074-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The MOsT common lesions that precede nonbullous impetigo are","o":["scabies","burns","insect bites","chickenpox","pediculosis 540 [Continuation on source page 551]"],"k":2,"ex":"Non-bullous impetigo commonly starts in a previous insect bite or skin break.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n74.(C).\n\nOptions:\nA,B,D,E are less common precursors.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 550 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":74,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-075-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome is caused predominantly by phage group 2 staphylococci, particularly strains 71 and 55, which are present at localized sites of infection. Of the following, the MosT common focus of infection is","o":["nasopharynx","umbilicus","urinary tract","superficial abrasion","conjunctivae"],"k":0,"ex":"SSSS is toxin-mediated; the most common focus of staphylococci is the nasopharynx.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n75.(A). Foci of infection include the nasopharynx and, less commonly, the umbilicus,\nurinary tract, a superficial abrasion, conjunctivae, and blood.\n\nOptions:\nB–E are less common.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 551 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":75,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-076-3","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Ecthyma resembles nonbullous impetigo in onset and appearance but gradually evolves into a deeper, more chronic infection. Of the following, the usual causative agent is","o":["S. aureus","S. pneumoniae","H. influenzae type b","Clostridium perfringens","group A-β hemolytic streptococcus (GABHS)"],"k":4,"ex":"Ecthyma is a deeper impetigo, usually due to group A β-haemolytic streptococcus.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n76.(E). The causative agent is usually GABHS; S. aureus is also cultured from most\nlesions but is probably a secondary pathogen.\n551\n[Source page 562]\n\nOptions:\nA is secondary; B–D unrelated.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"⚠ S. aureus also grown; key per Nelson","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 551 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":76,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-080-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following matching are true EXCEPT","o":["Tinea capitis: dermatophyte infection of the scalp","Tinea corporis: infection of the glabrous skin","Tinea cruris: infection of the axilla","Tinea pedis: infection of the toe webs","Tinea unguium: dermatophyte infection of the nail plate"],"k":2,"ex":"Tinea cruris is groin infection; the axilla is not the site.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n80.(C). Tinea cruris, or infection of the groin, occurs most often in adolescent males and\nis usually caused by the anthropophilic species Epidermophyton floccosum or T.\nrubrum, but occasionally by the zoophilic species T. mentagrophytes.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,D,E are correct.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 552 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":80,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-081-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A 3-month-old infant presented with an intensely erythematous, confluent plaque with a scalloped border, a sharply demarcated edge, and satellite pustules in the perianal skin, perineum, and inguinal folds. Of the following, the MOsT likely diagnosis is","o":["seborrheic dermatitis","atopic dermatitis","primary irritant contact dermatitis","candidal diaper dermatitis","bacterial dermatitis"],"k":3,"ex":"Candida nappy rash: red confluent plaque with satellite pustules in skin folds.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n81.(D).\n\nOptions:\nIrritant dermatitis spares folds; seborrhoeic/atopic lack satellites.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 552 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":81,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-082-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A 4-year-old child presented with a 2 pearly, skin-colored, smooth, dome-shaped, 4 mm papules with a central umbilication on the face. Of the following, the MOsT likely diagnosis is","o":["molluscum contagiosum","ectopic sebaceous glands","keratoacanthoma","warty dyskeratoma","cryptococcosis"],"k":0,"ex":"Pearly umbilicated papules on the face are molluscum (pox virus).","more":"Nelson explanation:\n82.(A). They typically have a central umbilication from which a plug of cheesy material\ncan be expressed. The papules may occur anywhere on the body, but the face, eyelids,\nneck, axillae, and thighs are sites of predilection.\n\nOptions:\nB–E do not have central umbilication with cheesy plug.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 552 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":82,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-083-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"All the following can be used in the treatment of papular urticaria ExCEPT","o":["oral antihistamines","cool compresses","potent topical corticosteroids","topical antihistamines","systemic steroids"],"k":3,"ex":"Papular urticaria (insect bites) is treated symptomatically; topical antihistamines are sensitisers.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n83.(D). Topical antihistamines are potent immunologic sensitizers and have no role in\nthe treatment of insect bite reactions.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,C,E are used.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 552 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":83,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-084-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Scabies is transmitted only rarely by fomites because the isolated mite dies within","o":["2-3 hours",". 12-24 hours","2-3 days","5-7 days","10-20 days"],"k":2,"ex":"The mite survives 2–3 days off the host, so fomite spread is rare.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n84.(C).\n\nOptions:\nA is too short; D,E too long.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 552 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":84,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-085-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"In an immunocompetent host, scabies is frequently heralded by intense pruritus, particularly at night. 542 [Continuation on source page 553] Of the following, the classic lesions of scabies are","o":["bullae","pustules","wheals","red papules","threadlike burrows"],"k":4,"ex":"Burrows are the pathognomonic scabies lesion.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n85.(E). Threadlike burrows are the classic lesion of scabies but may not be seen in\ninfants.\n\nOptions:\nA–D are non-specific.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 552 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":85,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-086","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"The treatment of choice for scabies is","o":["oral ivermectin","lindane 1% cream","sulfur ointment 5-10%","permethrin 5% cream","crotamiton 10% cream"],"k":3,"ex":"Permethrin 5% cream is first-line; all contacts are treated.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n86.(D). The treatment of choice for scabies is permethrin 5% cream (Elimite) applied to\nthe entire body from the neck down, with particular attention to intensely involved\nareas, which is also standard therapy.\n\nOptions:\nA ivermectin is alternative; B lindane toxic; C,E less effective.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 553 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":86,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-088-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Comedonal acne, particularly of the central face, is frequently the first sign of pubertal maturation. It occurs in","o":["20% of adolescents","40% of adolescents","60% of adolescents .80% of adolescents","100% of adolescents"],"k":3,"ex":"Comedonal acne is an early sign of puberty and affects most adolescents (~80%).","more":"Nelson explanation:\n88.(D).\n\nOptions:\nPercentages only.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 553 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":88,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-089-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following drugs can induce acneiform lesions in susceptible individuals EXCEPT","o":["gold","isoniazid","phenytoin","vitamin B1","phenobarbital"],"k":3,"ex":"Drugs causing acneiform eruptions include steroids, gold, isoniazid, phenytoin, phenobarbital; vitamin B1 is not one.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n89.(D). Diagnosis of acne is rarely difficult, although flat warts, folliculitis, and other\ntypes of acne (drug induced: glucocorticoidagents, anabolic steroids, gold,\ndactinomycin, isoniazid, lithium, phenytoin, progestins) may be confused with acne\nvulgaris. The differential diagnosis includes sarcoidosis, angiofibromas, keratosis pilaris,\nchloracne, rosacea, and fibrofolliculomas.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,C,E can cause acne.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"⚠ Possible OCR misread (vitamin B12 does cause acne); check PDF","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 553 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":89,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-090-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Little evidence shows that ingestion of the following foods can trigger acne flares","o":["high carbohydrate foods",".high spicy foods","high sweet foods","high fatty foods 543 [Continuation on source page 554]","no certain foods"],"k":4,"ex":"There is little evidence that foods trigger acne.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n90.(E). Little evidence shows that ingestion of particular foods can trigger acne flares.\nWhen a patient is convinced that certain dietary items exacerbate acne, it is prudent\nfor the patient to omit those foods.\n\nOptions:\nA–D are unproven triggers.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 553 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":90,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-091","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All topical preparations for acne vulgaris must be used for 6-8 wk before their effectiveness can be assessed. Of the following, the primary topical preparation should be","o":["azelaic acid","salicylic acid","topical retinoid","Benzoyl peroxide","topical clindamycin"],"k":2,"ex":"Topical retinoids are the primary acne therapy per Nelson.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n91.(C). A topical retinoid should be the primary treatment for acne vulgaris.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,D,E are adjuncts.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"⚠ Many guidelines also use benzoyl peroxide first-line","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 554 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":91,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-092","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Antibiotics are indicated for treatment of patients whose acne has not responded to topical medications. Of the following, the antibiotic that may cause bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes is","o":["tetracycline","doxycycline","minocycline","erythromycin","trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"],"k":2,"ex":"Minocycline can cause blue-grey skin/mucosal pigmentation, intracranial hypertension and lupus-like syndrome.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n92.(C). Rarely, minocycline causes dizziness, intracranial hypertension, bluish\ndiscoloration of the skin and mucous membranes, hepatitis, a lupus-like syndrome, and\ndrug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms.\n\nOptions:\nOthers lack this pigmentation.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 554 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":92,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-093","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Approximately 20% of normal neonates demonstrate acne in the 1t mo of life.Treatment is usually unnecessary. If desired, the lesions can be treated effectively with","o":["azelaic acid",".salicylic acid","topical retinoid","benzoyl peroxide","topical clindamycin"],"k":3,"ex":"Neonatal acne is mild and often untreated; benzoyl peroxide or antifungal can be used.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n93.(D). Topical antifungals and/or benzoyl peroxide.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,C,E are less used.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 554 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":93,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-096-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are features of acrodermatitis enteropathica ExCEPT","o":["a rare autosomal recessive disorder","caused by an inability to absorb sufficient zinc from the diet","Initial signs and symptoms usually occur in the 2nd year of life","the cutaneous eruption consists of vesiculobullous, eczematous, dry, scaly, skin lesions","alopecia"],"k":2,"ex":"AE is autosomal recessive zinc malabsorption with onset in the first months, often after weaning.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n96.(C). Initial signs and symptoms usually occur in the 1st few mo of life, often after\nweaning from breast milk to cow's milk.\n\nOptions:\nA,B,D,E are features; C is wrong.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 555 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":96,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-097","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Ocular manifestations of acrodermatitis enteropathica include all the following EXCEPT","o":["photophobia","cataract","conjunctivitis","blepharitis","corneal dystrophy"],"k":1,"ex":"Eye signs: photophobia, conjunctivitis, blepharitis, corneal dystrophy; cataract is not.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n97.(B).\n\nOptions:\nA,C,D,E occur.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 555 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":97,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-098-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The replacement of elemental zinc for individuals with inherited acrodermatitis enteropathica is equal to","o":["1 mg/kg/24 hr","3 mg/kg/24 hr","5 mg/kg/24 hr","7 mg/kg/24 hr","10 mg/kg/24 hr"],"k":1,"ex":"Oral zinc is lifelong treatment at about 3 mg/kg/day elemental zinc.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n98.(B). Oral therapy with zinc compounds is the treatment of choice. Replacement for\nindividuals with inherited acrodermatitis enteropathica is with 3 mg/kg/24 hr of\nelemental zinc found in zinc sulfate, gluconate, or acetate (i.e., 220 mg of zinc sulfate\ncontains 50 mg of elemental zinc). Zinc gluconate carries less risk of gastrointestinal\ndistress.\n\nOptions:\nOther doses are wrong.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 555 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":98,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-099-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The hair is sparse, thin, and depigmented, sometimes displaying a \"flag sign \"which is a characteristic finding in","o":["pellagra","scurvy","vitamin a deficiency","kwashiorkor","acrodermatitis enteropathica"],"k":3,"ex":"Sparse, depigmented hair with the flag sign (alternating bands) occurs in kwashiorkor.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n99.(D). Nails are thin and soft, and hair is sparse, thin, and depigmented, sometimes\ndisplaying a \"flag sign\" consisting of alternating light and dark bands that reflect\nalternating periods of adequate and inadequate nutrition.\n\nOptions:\nPellagra, scurvy, vitamin A, AE lack the flag sign.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 555 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":99,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-100-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Facial eruption that frequently follows a butterfly distribution and a dermatitis encircling the neck \"Casal's necklace.\" is usually seen in","o":["pellagra","scurvy","vitamin A deficiency","kwashiorkor 545 [Continuation on source page 556]","acrodermatitis enteropathica"],"k":0,"ex":"Pellagra (niacin) causes dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia; Casal's necklace is typical.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n100.(A).\n\nOptions:\nB–E have different skin signs.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 555 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":100,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DERMATOLOG-101-2","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The Most common risk factor for scurvy in children is","o":["prolonged antibiotics use","psychiatric disease","exclusive breast feeding","chronic diarrhea","cow milk protein allergy 546"],"k":1,"ex":"The commonest scurvy risk in children is poor nutrition from behavioural/psychiatric disorders.","more":"Nelson explanation:\n101.(B). In children, the most common risk factors are behavioralor psychiatric disease\nthat results in poor nutrition.\n553\n\nOptions:\nA,C,D,E are minor.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · The Skin · question page 556 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":101,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-011","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"Building a tower of 6 cubes by a 22-month-old child requires","o":["visualmotor coordination","ability uses objects in combination","visual, gross, and fine motor coordination","able to link actions to solve problems","symbolic thought"],"k":2,"ex":"Stacking cubes is a fine-motor-adaptive milestone: a tower of 2 cubes at ~15 months, 3–4 at 18 months, 6–7 at 24 months, 8–9 at 30 months and 10 at 36 months. Building a tower of 6 needs eye–hand (visual–fine motor) coordination together with trunk and arm stability (gross motor control) – the keyed answer combines these three components.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Visual-motor coordination alone: necessary but incomplete; it is a subset of option C.\n\n- **B.** Ability to use objects in combination: the cognitive skill shown by stacking/nesting objects, but not what the keyed item emphasizes.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer): visual, gross and fine motor coordination together.\n- **D.** Ability to link actions to solve problems: a cognitive (problem-solving) skill of the second year, e.g. using a tool.\n- **E.** Symbolic thought: pretend play and representational use of objects, not required for stacking.\n\nNelson explanation:\n11 (C).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (mild): the stem's discriminating idea ('requires …') is not explained in the source key; option A is contained in option C. Key preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Growth, Development, and Behavior · question page 19 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":11,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-013","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"You are observing a 15-month-old toddler, he was able to do all the following EXCEPT","o":["walks alone","makes tower of 3 cubes","inserts raisin in a bottle","responds to his/her name","identifies 1 or more parts of body"],"k":4,"ex":"At 15 months a toddler walks alone, can put small objects (raisin) into a bottle, builds a tower of two cubes, and responds to his name. Identifying body parts (pointing to 3 parts), 6–10 words and a tower of 3–4 cubes are 18-month skills.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Walks alone: expected by 12–15 months.\n\n- **B.** Tower of 3 cubes: by many tables (including SHETS) an 18-month skill; 2 cubes at 15 months – this makes the item debatable.\n- **C.** Inserts raisin in a bottle: a 14–15 month fine-motor skill.\n- **D.** Responds to name: present by 6–9 months.\n- **E.** Correct (source answer): identifying body parts is an 18-month skill (key).\n\nNelson explanation:\n13 (E). This is an 18 months developmental skill.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required (moderate): by SHETS, both options B (tower of 3) and E (body parts) are 18-month items, so the EXCEPT stem may have two exceptions; recommend moving to NEEDS REVIEW or correcting option B at the PDF. Source answer E preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Growth, Development, and Behavior · question page 19 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":13,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-014","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The age by which the child can makes tower of 9 cubes and imitates circular stroke is","o":["24 mo","30 mo","36 mo","42 mo","48 mo"],"k":1,"ex":"At about 30 months a child builds a tower of 8–9 cubes, imitates a circular stroke (draws vertical and horizontal strokes), goes up stairs alternating feet, uses the pronoun 'I', knows his full name, and helps put things away. At 24 months a tower of 6–7 cubes is built; at 36 months, 10 cubes and copying a circle.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** 24 months: tower of 6–7 cubes, lines imitated.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer): 30 months – tower of 9 cubes and imitates a circular stroke.\n- **C.** 36 months: tower of 10 cubes, copies a circle, imitates a cross.\n- **D.** 42 months: copies a cross/square-like figures, hops.\n- **E.** 48 months: draws a person (3–4 parts), copies a cross, tower skills no longer used.\n\nNelson explanation:\n14 (B). A 30 months old child also able to goes up stairs alternating feet, makes vertical\nand horizontal strokes, refers to self by pronoun \"I\"; knows full name, helps put things\naway and pretends in play.\n15 (8). A major milestone is the achievement by 9 mo of object permanence\n(constancy), the understanding that objects continue to exist, even when not seen. At\n4-7 month of age, infants look down for ball that has been dropped but quickly give up\nif it is not seen. With object constancy, infants persist in searching. They will find\nobjects hidden under a cloth or behind the examiner's back.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Source key block also contains text of another answer ('15 (8)… object permanence'), which belongs to Q15 and is not part of this question.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Growth, Development, and Behavior · question page 19 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":14,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-016","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The child who's able to stands momentarily on 1 foot, makes tower of 10 cubes; imitates construction of \"bridge\" of 3 cubes; copies circle and imitates cross is","o":["24 mo old handedness is achieved by 3 years of age","30 mo old 9 [Continuation on source page 20] boys are usually later than girls in achieving bladder control","36 mo old tries to reduce pressures associated with speaking knowing gender by 4 years","42 mo old I'll refer him to ENT specialist for further evaluation egocentric thinking","48 mo old visited outpatients department. Of the following, the MosT appropriate advice is I'l refer him to speech therapist EXCEPT musturbation"],"k":2,"ex":"At 36 months (3 years) a child stands momentarily on one foot, builds a tower of 10 cubes, imitates a three-cube bridge, copies a circle and imitates a cross, rides a tricycle and speaks in 3-word sentences. At 24 months she runs and builds a 6-cube tower; at 30 months a 9-cube tower; at 42–48 months she hops, copies a cross and draws a person.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** 24 months: tower of 6–7, runs, 2–3 word phrases.\n\n- **B.** 30 months: tower of 9, circular stroke, alternate-foot stairs.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer): 36 months – balances momentarily on one foot, tower of 10, bridge, circle, imitates cross.\n- **D.** 42 months: hops, copies a cross, draws a person with 3 parts.\n- **E.** 48 months: copies a square, hops, catches a ball, counts to 10.\n\nNelson explanation:\n16 (C).\n15\n[Source page 26]","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR contamination: after the page marker the block contains fragments of other questions (a stuttering advice question and a pre-school development 'EXCEPT' question: handedness, bladder control, gender, egocentric thinking, masturbation). They are not part of Q16 and were ignored. Check PDF pages 19–20.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Growth, Development, and Behavior · question page 19 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":16,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-019","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"A worried mother of a 4-year-old boy describing attacks of inconsolable crying episodes of her child, taking long time, she stated also that he prefers to play alone. Of the following, the MOsT appropriate action is to","o":["reassures her that this is a normal phenomenon of temper tantrum","seek more history regarding other skills and developmental domains","refer her to pediatric psychiatry","investigate social issues of the family","investigate for the child abuse"],"k":1,"ex":"Tantrums are normal from the end of the first year and peak at 2–4 years; they are caused by fear, tiredness, frustration, inconsistent expectations or discomfort. Red flags are tantrums lasting more than 15 minutes, more than 3 per day, severe aggression/self-injury, or association with other developmental concerns.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Reassure that this is normal: wrong, because duration and social isolation are red flags.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer): seek more history on other skills and developmental domains (screen for autism spectrum or other disorders).\n- **C.** Refer to pediatric psychiatry: appropriate later if the assessment suggests a disorder, but not the first step.\n- **D.** Investigate family social issues: part of a full assessment, but not specific to this presentation.\n- **E.** Investigate for child abuse: nothing in the story suggests abuse.\n\nNelson explanation:\n19 (B). Classical temper tantrums of children usually result from fear, overtiredness,\ninconsistent expectations, or physical discomfort. Tantrums normally appear toward\nthe end of the 1st yr of life and peak in prevalence between 2 and 4 years of age.\nTantrums lasting more than 15 min or regularly occurring more than 3 times/day may\nreflect underlying medical, emotional, or social problems. in the scenario the episodes\ntakes long time to resolve and the mother points to problem in social development.\nHere we need to investigate the developmental domains and to do systemic\nexamination to checkout for any developmental/physical problem before referring the\nchild to pediatric psychiatrist which the next most appropriate choice.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Growth, Development, and Behavior · question page 20 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":19,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-022","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A medical student asked you during the morning round about his observation of infants cry in response to the cry of another infant. Of the following, the MosT appropriate answer is that it represent","o":["an early sign of empathy development","a sign of good hearing","a startle reflex to a loud sound","an early sign of fear development","an early sign of autistic behavior"],"k":0,"ex":"Young infants often cry when they hear another infant cry ('reactive' or contagious crying). This is regarded as an early sign of empathy (emotional resonance), usually observed in the first months.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer): early sign of developing empathy.\n\n- **B.** Sign of good hearing: it shows hearing, but the key meaning is empathy; the question asks for the 'most appropriate' interpretation.\n- **C.** Startle (Moro) reflex: a response to loud noise or sudden head movement, not to another baby's crying.\n- **D.** Early sign of fear: fear of strangers appears about 6–9 months.\n- **E.** Early sign of autistic behavior: autism shows reduced social responsiveness, not crying with others.\n\nNelson explanation:\n22 (A).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source answer key gives the letter only.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Growth, Development, and Behavior · question page 21 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":22,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-007","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A 7-year-old boy presented with recurrent eye blinking behavior and recurrent extenslon of extremities, mother describe the movement as sudden, rapid, and repetitive movements, it was present in the last 9 months. Of the following, the MosT appropriate diagnosis is","o":["Tourette's disorder","persistent motor tic disorder","provisional tic disorder","post-viral encephalitis","Sydenham chorea"],"k":2,"ex":"Tics are sudden, rapid, recurrent, non-rhythmic, stereotyped movements or vocalizations (eye blinking, neck jerks, shoulder shrugging, limb extension) with onset at 4–6 years. Classification: provisional (transient) tic disorder – tics present for less than 1 year; persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder – more than 1 year; Tourette disorder – multiple motor plus at least one vocal tic for more than 1 year.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Tourette disorder: needs multiple motor and ≥1 vocal tic for more than 1 year.\n\n- **B.** Persistent motor tic disorder: tics for more than 1 year.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer): provisional tic disorder (duration <1 year; 9 months here).\n- **D.** Post-viral encephalitis: would present with encephalopathy, fever/seizures, not isolated stereotyped tics.\n- **E.** Sydenham chorea: post-streptococcal, with random, non-repetitive movements, hypotonia and emotional lability (OCR: option letter lost).\n\nNelson explanation:\n7.(C). A tic is a sudden, rapid, recurrent (in the same manner), non-rhythmic motor,\nmovement or vocalization e.g. eye blinking, neck jerking, shoulder shrugging, extension\nof the extremities. Onset of tics is typically between ages 4 and 6 yr, in Tourette's\ndisorder both multiple motor and one or more vocal tics have been present at some\ntime during the illness, may be concurrently for > 1year. In chronic or persistent tic\ndisorder either one or more motor or vocal tics are present during the course of the\ndisease while in provisional tic disorders the duration is < 1 yr. Chorea is involuntary,\nrandom, quick, jerking movements, most often of the proximal extremities, that flow\nfrom joint to joint (not in same manner), movements are abrupt, non-repetitive, and\narrhythmic and have variable frequency and intensity.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Behavior and Psychiatric Disorders · question page 30 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":7,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-018","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A concerned young parents asking about frequent lying behavior experienced by their 3-year-old girl. The following advices are true ExCEPT","o":["it is a method of playing with the language","it is a part of their magical thinking","it indicates a potential for future lying behavior","it is an approach to avoid unwanted confrontation with adults","it is a way to describe things as they wish"],"k":2,"ex":"Lying is common and normal between 2 and 4 years: children play with language, mix fantasy and reality (magical thinking), say what they wish was true, and try to avoid unwanted adult reactions. It does not predict future pathological lying; parents should respond calmly and model honesty.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Playing with the language: true.\n\n- **B.** Part of magical thinking: true.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): it does not indicate a potential for future lying behavior (key).\n- **D.** Way to avoid unwanted confrontation: true.\n- **E.** Way to describe things as they wish: true.\n\nNelson explanation:\n18.(C). Lying is a normal behavior between 2-4 years and represents all the above\nexcept the option C.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Behavior and Psychiatric Disorders · question page 32 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":18,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-024","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Diagnosis of autistic spectrum disorder (ASD) depends partly but importantly on assessment of language. All the following may raise your concern regarding language development and may indicate ASD EXCEPT","o":["absent babbling by 6 months","absent gestures by 12 month","absent single words by 16 month","absent 2-word purposeful phrases by 24 month","loss of language or social skills at any time"],"k":0,"ex":"Language assessment is central to recognizing autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Language red flags: no babbling by 12 months, no gestures (pointing, waving) by 12 months, no single words by 16 months, no spontaneous 2-word phrases by 24 months, and any loss of language or social skills at any age.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): the red flag is absence of babbling by 12 months, not 6 months (key).\n\n- **B.** No gestures by 12 months: red flag.\n- **C.** No single words by 16 months: red flag.\n- **D.** No 2-word phrases by 24 months: red flag.\n- **E.** Loss of language or social skills at any age: red flag (regression).\n\nNelson explanation:\n24.(A). Absence of babbling by 12 months is a red flag for language development and\nshould raise a suspicion of AsD (autistic spectrum disorder).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Behavior and Psychiatric Disorders · question page 33 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":24,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-032","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Childhood psychosis may include all the following ExCEPT","o":["delusions","loss of reality testing","disorganized speech","catatonic behavior","acute phobic hallucination"],"k":4,"ex":"Psychosis in childhood includes delusions, hallucinations with loss of reality testing, disorganized speech/behavior, and catatonia. Acute phobic hallucinations are brief, benign, fear-related hallucinations seen in previously healthy preschoolers; mental status is otherwise normal, so they are NOT psychotic.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Delusions: core psychotic symptom.\n\n- **B.** Loss of reality testing: defining feature of psychosis.\n- **C.** Disorganized speech: a psychotic symptom (schizophrenia criteria).\n- **D.** Catatonic behavior: part of psychotic disorders.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): acute phobic hallucination is a normal/non-psychotic phenomenon of preschoolers.\n\nNelson explanation:\n32.(E).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source key gives the letter only.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Behavior and Psychiatric Disorders · question page 35 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":32,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-033","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A parents came to your clinic complaining that their 5-year-old boy had attacks of frightening with imagination of a snake crawling over him and he is acting as trying to remove it. You assessed the child and find no acute physical illness and he is cooperative and quite intelligent. Of the following, the MosT appropriate explanation for the child behavior is","o":["acute phobic hallucination","early sign of schizophrenia","delusional infestation","night terror","bipolar disorder 25"],"k":0,"ex":"A previously healthy, intelligent preschooler is frightened by a vivid image of a snake on him and tries to remove it; he is cooperative and has no abnormal mental state. This fits an acute phobic hallucination: benign, common in preschool children, usually visual or tactile, lasting 1–3 days and settling over 1–2 weeks.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer): acute phobic hallucination – benign and self-limited.\n\n- **B.** Early sign of schizophrenia: rare at this age and associated with abnormal mental status and other symptoms.\n- **C.** Delusional infestation: fixed false belief of being infested; a psychotic disorder of adults.\n- **D.** Night terror: arises from deep sleep, the child is inconsolable and amnesic; this child is awake and cooperative.\n- **E.** Bipolar disorder: needs mood episodes; not suggested.\n\nNelson explanation:\n33.(A). In children with non-psychotic hallucinations, the symptoms of psychosis are\nabsent; normal mental status examination is the key for it is diagnosis. Non-psychotic\nhallucinations commonly occur in the context of severe traumatic stress,\ndevelopmental difficulties, social and emotional deprivation. Hallucinations related to\nfantasy, culture believes, grief, hypnogogic hallucinations, night terrors, fever, and\nacute phobic hallucinations all are considered normal. In this scenario, the case\ntypically has an acute phobic hallucination which is benign, common and occurs in\npreviously healthy preschool children. The hallucinations are often visual or tactile, last\n29\n[Source page 40]\nSymptoms can persist for 1-3 days, slowly abating over 1-2 wk.\n30","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: page markers ('25', '29', '[Source page 40]', '30') are interleaved inside the source explanation.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Behavior and Psychiatric Disorders · question page 35 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":33,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-002","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The neurodevelopmental function (cognition) is dependent mainly on the development of the following functions ExcEPT","o":["sensory and motor","language","visual-spatial","intellectual","cerebeller"],"k":4,"ex":"Neurodevelopmental function (cognition) is the basic brain process needed for learning and productivity. It depends on sensory and motor function, language, visual-spatial skills, intellectual ability, memory, social cognition and frontal-lobe functions (attention and executive function).","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Sensory and motor: a basic component.\n\n- **B.** Language: a core component.\n- **C.** Visual-spatial skills: a core component.\n- **D.** Intellectual (general cognitive) function: a core component.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT, per key): cerebellar function is not listed by Nelson (the key stresses frontal-lobe functions).\n\nNelson explanation:\n2.(E). Neurodevelopmental function is a basic brain process needed for learning and\nproductivity. It needs all that process that mentioned above plus memory, social\ncognition and frontal lobe functions. The process of attention and executive\nfunctioning is heavily dependent on frontal lobe function.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (mild): the cerebellum has recognized cognitive roles, so the keyed exception rests on Nelson's wording. Source answer preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Learning Disorders · question page 41 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":2,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-003","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which statement is FALSE regarding specific learning disorders (SLD)?","o":["it is a type of neurodevelopmental dysfunctions","the overall estimates of the prevalence of SLD range from 3-10%","it focuses on academic skill development","Includes students did wellin academic testing but not in intelligence testing","terms as dysgraphia or dyscalculia had been revolutionized"],"k":3,"ex":"Specific learning disorders (SLD) are neurodevelopmental disorders affecting academic skills (reading, spelling, arithmetic, written language) with prevalence of about 3–10%. The defining feature is a discrepancy between normal intelligence and poor academic achievement.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** True: SLD are neurodevelopmental disorders.\n\n- **B.** True per the key: prevalence estimates about 3–10%.\n- **C.** True: they concern academic skill development.\n- **D.** Correct answer (FALSE statement): SLD students do well on IQ testing but poorly on academic achievement tests (OCR: option letter lost).\n- **E.** Wording unclear in OCR ('revolutionized'); the source says use of the terms dyscalculia and dysgraphia in diagnostic systems has been inconsistent.\n\nNelson explanation:\n3.(D). Specific developmental disorders of scholastic Skills (specific learning disorders)\nincludes reading disorder, spelling disorder, disorder of arithmetical skills, and mixed\ndisorder of Scholastic Skills. Dyslexia (reading disorder) is included in ICD-10 in a\nseparate category of symbolic dysfunction. The terms, Dyscalculia (mathematics\ndisorder), and Dysgraphia (written language disorder) are also used by investigators\nand clinicians, but their inclusion in diagnostic classification systems has been\ninconsistent and a source of some disagreement among experts. A student exhibiting a\nsignificant discrepancy between scores on tests of intelligence and tests of academic\nachievement could be classified as a student with an SlD, and would subsequently be\neligible for Special Education Services i.e. those student may did very well in lQ testing\nbut not in academic achievement or tests.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Option E wording looks garbled by OCR/translation ('revolutionized'); verify against the PDF.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Learning Disorders · question page 41 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":3,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-DEVELOPMEN-012","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A 3-month-precious baby of a keen and highly educated family presented with history of URTl followed by deterioration of level of consciousness and seizure. CT brain shows intracranial bleeding with no evidence of skull fracture. Fundocscopy was normal. Mother gives history of difficult labour but with normal development, mild hypotonia, and macrocephaly which was reassured initially by general Pediatrician. Of the following, the MOsT likely diagnosis is","o":["residual birth trauma","arteriovenous malformation","acute viral encephalitis","glutaric aciduria type 1","abusive head trauma (AHT)"],"k":3,"ex":"A 3-month-old with macrocephaly, mild hypotonia, normal development, an infection-triggered encephalopathic crisis, seizures and intracranial (subdural) bleeding without skull fracture or retinal hemorrhage points to glutaric aciduria type 1 (GA1). GA1 causes macrocephaly, frontotemporal atrophy with wide Sylvian fissures and stretched bridging veins, so subdural hemorrhage can occur with minimal trauma; infection or fasting triggers striatal injury and dystonia.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Residual birth trauma: subdural bleeding from birth usually resolves in the first month.\n\n- **B.** Arteriovenous malformation: usually causes subarachnoid or parenchymal hemorrhage and would not explain macrocephaly with hypotonia.\n- **C.** Acute viral encephalitis: possible, but intracranial bleeding is unusual.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): glutaric aciduria type 1 – macrocephaly, hypotonia, subdural hemorrhage after a minor illness, no retinal hemorrhage.\n- **E.** Abusive head trauma: must be considered, but absent retinal hemorrhage and the macrocephaly/hypotonia make GA1 more likely.\n\nNelson explanation:\n12.(D). Patlents with birth trauma may have asymptomatic subdural hematoma which\nmay resolve by the end of first month of life. Arteriovenous malformation usually has\nsubarachnoid hemorrhage. Encephalitis is possibility but unusually present with\nIntracranial bleed. AHT is a possibility but presence of hypotonia and macrocephaly and\nabsence of retinal hemorrhage make the diagnosis of Glutaric aciduria type 1 is the\nmost llkely one.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: stem begins '3-month-precious baby' (probably 'precious' for 'previous' or 'premature'); wording kept as extracted. Verify against the PDF.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Children with Special Needs · question page 48 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":12,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-003","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"The following are main features of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) EXCEPT","o":["hyperglycemia (glucose usually >300 mg/dL)","ketonemia (serum ketones >3 mmol/L)","ketonuria","venous pH <7.20","serum HCO3<15 mEq/L"],"k":3,"ex":"DKA is defined by hyperglycemia, ketosis (ketones in blood and urine) and metabolic acidosis. The acidosis criterion is a venous pH below 7.30 with bicarbonate of 15 mEq/L or less.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nHyperglycemia is the first element (the curriculum uses a diagnostic threshold above 200 mg/dL, and values above 300 are typical at presentation). Ketonemia and ketonuria are the defining ketosis findings. A serum bicarbonate under 15 mEq/L matches the curriculum criterion (≤15). None of these is an exception to the DKA picture.\n\nHigh-yield:\nEuglycemic or only mildly hyperglycemic DKA can occur (for example, with SGLT2 inhibitors or prolonged vomiting), so ketones and pH, not glucose alone, make the diagnosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.446 (printed 436) · Answer PDF p.466","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":3,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-010","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","q":"Physiologic factors play a role in stimulating and inhibiting growth hormone (GH). One of the following inhibit GH release","o":["sleep","exercise","hyperglycemia","acute illness","fasting"],"k":2,"ex":"GH is secreted in pulses and is stimulated by stress, sleep, exercise, hypoglycemia and starvation, and by arginine and α-adrenergic stimulation. It is inhibited by hyperglycemia, somatostatin, β-adrenergic stimulation and by GH/IGF-1 negative feedback (and, per Nelson, by hypothyroidism and glucocorticoids).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nSleep, exercise, acute illness (a physical stress) and fasting/hypoglycemia all stimulate GH release. This is why provocation tests use insulin-induced hypoglycemia, glucagon, clonidine, arginine or exercise, and why a random GH level is of little value.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF pp.447–448 (printed 437–438) · Answer PDF p.466 (key printed as \"(c)\")","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":10,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-012","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MosT common cause of acquired hypopituitarism is","o":["craniopharyngioma","eosinophilic granuloma (histiocytosis)","tuberculosis","toxoplasmosis","meningitis"],"k":0,"ex":"Any lesion that damages the hypothalamus, pituitary stalk or anterior pituitary can cause hormone deficiency, and because such lesions are not selective, multiple deficiencies are usual. The most common lesion in children is craniopharyngioma, either from the tumor itself or from its surgery and radiotherapy.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nHistiocytosis (infiltration) and CNS infection such as meningitis are recognized acquired causes but are much less frequent. Tuberculosis and toxoplasmosis of the pituitary region are rare causes. All of them would become likelier with the right history (multisystem histiocytosis, previous meningitis, immunosuppression).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.448 (printed 438) · Answer PDF p.466","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":12,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-013","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Treatment · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Indications for GH treatment to promote growth include the following EXCEPT","o":["GH deficiency","chronic renal failure before transplantation","celiac disease","Prader-Willi syndrome","Noonan syndrome"],"k":2,"ex":"GH therapy is used for GH deficiency, Turner syndrome, chronic kidney disease before transplantation, Prader–Willi syndrome, idiopathic short stature, short stature after intrauterine growth restriction without catch-up, and SHOX deficiency. Growth failure in celiac disease is due to malabsorption and is treated by a gluten-free diet, not GH, so C is the exception.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nGH deficiency is the classic indication. CKD and Prader–Willi syndrome are established indications. Noonan syndrome is listed by Nelson as an approved indication (the curriculum marks it as controversial, but it is not the intended exception here).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.448 (printed 438) · Answer PDF p.466","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":13,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-014","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pharmacology · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Reported side effects of GH treatment include the following EXCEPT","o":["pseudotumor cerebri",".slipped capital femoral epiphysis `[label restored as B]`","gynecomastia","worsening of scoliosis `[label restored as D]`","6-fold increase in the risk for type 1 diabetes"],"k":4,"ex":"GH is anti-insulin (it raises insulin resistance), so the diabetes risk it carries is for type 2 diabetes. Nelson states that GH is associated with a 6-fold increase in type 2 diabetes and no significant increase in type 1 diabetes, so E is the false statement.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nPseudotumor cerebri, slipped capital femoral epiphysis and gynecomastia are listed GH side effects, as are increased insulin resistance and a (debated) leukemia risk in the curriculum. Worsening scoliosis is a recognized effect of rapid growth under GH (Nelson). These explain the monitoring needed during treatment (fundi, hips, spine, glucose).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.448 (printed 438) · Answer PDF p.467","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":14,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-020","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis · **Depth:** Short","q":"Precocious puberty in girls is defined by the onset of secondary sexual characteristics before the age of","o":["7yr","8yr","9yr","10 yr","11 yr"],"k":1,"ex":"Precocious puberty is the appearance of secondary sexual characteristics before 8 years in girls and before 9 years in boys. Normal puberty starts at 8–13 years in girls (breast budding first) and 9–14 years in boys (testicular enlargement first).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n7 years is below the accepted threshold, and 9, 10 and 11 years fall within or near the normal range for girls (9 years is the boys' cut-off). Using the wrong threshold would label normal early puberty as pathological or miss true precocity.\n\nHigh-yield:\nPrecocious puberty is usually idiopathic in girls but usually pathological in boys.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.449 (printed 439) · Answer PDF p.467","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":20,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-022","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (disease characteristics) · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"All the following are true in premature thelarche ExCEPT","o":["most often appears in the 1st 2 yr of life","growth and osseous maturation are usually normal","menarche occurs at the expected age",". may be unilateral `[label restored as D]`","often persists till puberty"],"k":4,"ex":"Premature thelarche is isolated breast development, usually between 6 months and 2 years, with no other sign of puberty and no growth spurt or bone-age advance. It is benign and self-limiting, so the breast tissue typically regresses over a few years rather than persisting until true puberty, which makes E the false statement.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nOnset in the first two years is typical. Growth velocity and bone age are normal, which helps separate it from central precocious puberty. Menarche and later puberty occur at the usual age. Breast enlargement may be asymmetrical or unilateral. Follow-up (and a bone age or pelvic ultrasound if there is doubt) is advised, because progression or other pubertal signs would suggest central precocious puberty.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF pp.449–450 (printed 439–440) · Answer PDF p.467","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":22,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-024","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","q":"The MOsT accurate test of thyroid function is","o":["T4","free T4","T3","thyroglobulin","TSH"],"k":4,"ex":"Serum TSH is the most sensitive and accurate single test of thyroid function in a child with an intact hypothalamic–pituitary axis. It is high in primary hypothyroidism and suppressed in hyperthyroidism, and it is the basis of newborn screening.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nTotal T4 and T3 are influenced by binding proteins and are less reliable. Free T4 is used with TSH to confirm the diagnosis and is the better test in central (secondary or tertiary) hypothyroidism, where TSH is low or inappropriately normal, so it would be preferred in that situation. Thyroglobulin is a marker of thyroid tissue (for example in dysgenesis or thyroid cancer follow-up), not a test of function.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.450 (printed 440) · Answer PDF p.467","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":24,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-027","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MosT common cause of permanent congenital hypothyroidism is","o":["dyshormonogenesis","thyroid dysgenesis","lodine deficiency","defect of iodide transport","Pendred syndrome"],"k":1,"ex":"Thyroid dysgenesis (agenesis, hypoplasia or an ectopic gland) is the commonest cause of permanent congenital hypothyroidism. It is usually sporadic and produces no goiter.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nDyshormonogenesis (autosomal recessive hormone-synthesis defects, including iodide-transport defects) is the second most common cause (about 10%) and is associated with goiter. Pendred syndrome is one of these (goiter, sensorineural deafness, pendrin gene mutation). Iodine deficiency is the commonest cause worldwide but is preventable with salt iodization and is not the usual permanent cause in screened populations.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF pp.450–451 (printed 440–441) · Answer PDF p.468","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":27,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-032","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Treatment · **Depth:** Short","q":"Levothyroxine (I-T4) given orally is the treatment of cholce In congenital hypothyroidism. The recommended initial starting dose is","o":["2-5 μg/kg/day","4-7 μg/kg/day","5-9 μg/kg/day","8-12 μg/kg/day","10-15 μg/kg/day"],"k":4,"ex":"The recommended starting dose in the neonate is 10–15 µg/kg/day of oral levothyroxine, usually 37.5–50 µg per day in a term infant. Rapid normalization of thyroid hormones, with treatment started within the first two weeks, is what protects neurodevelopment, and treatment is lifelong.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nThe lower ranges (2–9 µg/kg/day, or 8–12 which does not reach the full recommended range) would under-treat and delay normalization of TSH and T4. Doses are then adjusted by follow-up of growth, TFT, bone age and development.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF pp.451–452 (printed 441–442) · Answer PDF p.468","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":32,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-033","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MosT common cause of acquired hypothyroidism is","o":["craniopharyngioma","BHashimoto thyroiditis","meningoencephalitis","drug-induced","irradiation"],"k":1,"ex":"Chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis is the commonest cause of acquired hypothyroidism and of sporadic goiter in childhood and adolescence. It is autoimmune (anti-TPO and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies), commoner in girls and in the 10–18 year age group, and is associated with other autoimmune diseases and with Down and Turner syndromes.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nCraniopharyngioma, meningoencephalitis and irradiation damage the hypothalamus or pituitary and cause central (secondary or tertiary) hypothyroidism with a low or normal TSH, which is a different pattern. Drug-induced hypothyroidism (for example from antithyroid drugs or lithium) and the other acquired causes in the curriculum (subacute thyroiditis, thyroid surgery or radioiodine, tumor infiltration, iodine deficiency) are far less common.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.452 (printed 442) · Answer PDF p.468","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":33,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-047","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Laboratory findings in hypoparathyroidism include the following EXCEPT","o":["low serum calcium (5-7 mg/dL)","elevated serum phosphorus (7-12 mg/dL)","Celevated serum alkaline phosphatase","level of 1,25(OH)2D3 is usually low `[label restored as D]`","normal serum magnesium"],"k":2,"ex":"Without PTH there is hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia (reduced phosphate excretion) and low PTH. Bone turnover is not stimulated, so alkaline phosphatase is normal or low, not elevated.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nLow calcium and high phosphorus are the defining biochemical findings. Because PTH stimulates renal activation of vitamin D, calcitriol (1,25(OH)2D3) is low. Magnesium is normal in primary hypoparathyroidism; a low magnesium would instead be a separate cause of hypocalcemia (hypomagnesemia is one of the other causes of hypocalcemia in the curriculum).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.454 (printed 444) · Answer PDF p.469","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":47,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-055","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Patients with mineralocorticoid deficiency can develop the following EXCEPT A weight loss","o":["hypotension","metabolic alkalosis","hyponatremia","hyperkalemia"],"k":2,"ex":"Aldosterone retains sodium and water and excretes potassium and hydrogen ions. Its deficiency leads to salt and water loss (weight loss, dehydration, hypotension), hyponatremia, hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nWeight loss, hypotension (including postural hypotension), hyponatremia and hyperkalemia are all expected in Addison disease and in salt-losing congenital adrenal hyperplasia, and the blood gas shows metabolic acidosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.456 (printed 446) · Answer PDF p.469","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":55,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-056","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","q":"The MosT common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in infancy is","o":["congenital adrenal hyperplasia","isolated glucocorticoid deficiency","X-linked adrenal hypoplasia congenita","Lemli-opitz syndrome","adrenoleukodystrophy"],"k":0,"ex":"The salt-losing forms of congenital adrenal hyperplasia are the commonest cause of adrenal insufficiency in infancy. 21-hydroxylase deficiency accounts for more than 90% of CAH, and these infants cannot make cortisol or aldosterone, so they present with an adrenal crisis (vomiting, dehydration, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia, shock) in the first weeks of life.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nIsolated glucocorticoid deficiency, X-linked adrenal hypoplasia congenita and Lemli–Opitz syndrome are rare causes. X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy is a rare neurodegenerative metabolic disorder that usually causes adrenal insufficiency in boys later in childhood. They would be considered if CAH is excluded (normal 17-hydroxyprogesterone), especially with a family history, neurologic signs or dysmorphism.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.456 (printed 446) · Answer PDF pp.469–470","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":56,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-058","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MosT definitive test for adrenal insufficiency is measurement of","o":["blood sugar","cortisol before and after administration of ACTH `[label restored as B]`","serum sodium","arterial blood gases","urinary excretion of sodium and chloride"],"k":1,"ex":"The ACTH (Synacthen) stimulation test is definitive. In adrenal insufficiency, resting cortisol is low and does not rise normally after ACTH, and a normal response excludes the diagnosis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nLow blood sugar, low sodium, metabolic acidosis on blood gas and altered urinary sodium losses are supportive findings of an adrenal crisis but are non-specific and can occur in many conditions. They guide emergency treatment (saline, dextrose, hydrocortisone), whereas the stimulation test confirms the diagnosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.456 (printed 446) · Answer PDF p.470","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":58,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-073","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (disease characteristics) · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"Klinefelter syndrome is the most common sex chromosomal aneuploidy in males, all the following features are true ExCEPT","o":["80% of them have a 47,xxy chromosome complement","diagnosis is rarely made before puberty","psychiatric disorders may be apparent long before defects in sexual development","few complete high school","approximately 80% of adults have gynecomastia"],"k":3,"ex":"Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) presents with infertility, small testes, tall stature and gynecomastia in adolescence. Intelligence is usually in the normal range, though some boys have language-based learning and psychological problems.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nAccording to Nelson, about 80% have the pure 47,XXY complement (the rest are mosaics or have additional X chromosomes), the diagnosis is rarely made before puberty, behavioral and psychiatric problems can appear before sexual development is affected, and gynecomastia is common in adults. Options A, B, C and E go beyond the lecture sheet, but D is identifiable from the taught statement that intelligence is usually normal.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.459 (printed 449) · Answer PDF p.471","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":73,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-076","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short","q":"Significant gynecomastia is seen in","o":["hyperthyroidism","Klinefelter syndrome","17-ketosteroid reductase deficiency","11β-hydroxylase deficiency","Peutz-Jeghers syndrome"],"k":1,"ex":"Klinefelter syndrome causes testicular failure with a relative excess of estrogen to androgen, and gynecomastia in adolescence is a classic feature (with an increased risk of breast cancer later).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nHyperthyroidism can cause gynecomastia, and thyroid tests are part of the work-up, but it is not a \"significant\" association in the way Klinefelter is. The other options (17-ketosteroid reductase deficiency, 11β-hydroxylase deficiency, Peutz–Jeghers syndrome) are rare in this context and are not considered in routine gynecomastia teaching. They would be considered with ambiguous genitalia, hypertension or testicular tumor signs.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.460 (printed 450) · Answer PDF p.471","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":76,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-079","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (associations) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MOsT common cardiac defect in Turner syndrome is","o":["aortic coarctation","isolated nonstenotic bicuspid aortic valves `[label restored as B]`","aortic stenosis","mitral valve prolapse anomalous pulmonary venous drainage `[label restored as E]`"],"k":1,"ex":"The commonest cardiac abnormality in Turner syndrome (45,X) is a bicuspid aortic valve, found in about one third to one half of patients. Coarctation of the aorta comes next (about 15–20%).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nCoarctation is the second most frequent lesion and the classic \"left-sided\" association, but it is less common than the bicuspid valve. Aortic stenosis, mitral valve prolapse and anomalous pulmonary venous drainage are less frequent. Pulmonary valve stenosis is the typical defect in Noonan syndrome, not Turner.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.460 (printed 450) · Answer PDF p.472","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":79,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-081","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (associations) · **Depth:** Short","q":"The MosT common cardiac defect in Noonan syndrome is","o":["aortic coarctation","pulmonary valvular stenosis `[label restored as B]`","aortic stenosis","mitral valve prolapse","anomalous pulmonary venous drainage"],"k":1,"ex":"Noonan syndrome (autosomal dominant, normal karyotype, both sexes) most often has pulmonary valve stenosis, with atrial septal defect and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy also common. It resembles Turner syndrome in webbed neck and short stature, but the cardiac lesions are right-sided rather than left-sided.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nCoarctation and aortic valve disease are the typical left-sided lesions of Turner syndrome. Mitral valve prolapse and anomalous pulmonary venous drainage are not characteristic of Noonan syndrome.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.461 (printed 451) · Answer PDF p.472","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":81,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-082","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis · **Depth:** Short","q":"Familial isolated gonadotropin deficiency associated with anosmia is","o":["Noonan syndrome","Bloom syndrome","Werner syndrome","Kallmann syndrome","Prader-Willi"],"k":3,"ex":"Kallmann syndrome is GnRH deficiency with absent or reduced sense of smell. It causes hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, so puberty is delayed and LH and FSH are low.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nNoonan syndrome (normal karyotype, autosomal dominant) has short stature, webbed neck and pulmonary stenosis, with delayed puberty and undescended testes but no isolated gonadotropin deficiency with anosmia. Prader–Willi syndrome has hypotonia, hyperphagia and obesity with hypogonadism, but not anosmia. Bloom syndrome (growth deficiency, sun-sensitive rash, cancer risk) and Werner syndrome (adult-onset premature aging) are unrelated to puberty.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.461 (printed 451) · Answer PDF p.472 (key printed as \"82.(D).\" with no explanation)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":82,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-084","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis · **Depth:** Short","q":"The current criteria for the diagnosis of type 1 and type 2 DM is a fasting blood glucose that exceeds","o":["110 mg/dL","115 mg/dL","120 mg/dL","125 mg/dL","130 mg/dL"],"k":3,"ex":"Diabetes is diagnosed by a fasting plasma glucose above 125 mg/dL (≥126). In a symptomatic child, a random blood sugar ≥200 mg/dL or a 2-hour post-prandial or OGTT value ≥200 mg/dL also establishes the diagnosis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n110, 115 and 120 mg/dL fall in the impaired-fasting-glucose range rather than the diabetic range, and 130 mg/dL is above the threshold but is not the criterion.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.461 (printed 451) · Answer PDF p.472","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":84,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-091","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis · **Depth:** Short","q":"Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) may be arbitrarily classified as mild, moderate, or severe. Venous pH in severe DKA is","o":["<7.05","<7.10","<7.15","<7.20","<7.25"],"k":2,"ex":"DKA is graded by the degree of acidosis. Severe DKA is a venous pH below 7.15 (with bicarbonate below 5 mmol/L), moderate DKA is below 7.25 (bicarbonate below 10), and mild DKA is below 7.3 (bicarbonate below 15).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nCut-offs of 7.20 and 7.25 correspond to moderate disease, and 7.05 and 7.10 are lower than the accepted severe threshold.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.462 (printed 452) · Answer PDF p.473 (key printed as \"91.(C).\" with no explanation)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":91,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-098","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation · **Depth:** Short","q":"Guidelines suggest that diabetic patients should commence screening for celiac disease","o":["at diagnosis","1 yr after diagnosis `[label restored as B]`","3 yr after diagnosis","5 yr after diagnosis","10 yr after diagnosis"],"k":0,"ex":"Type 1 diabetes shares autoimmune genetics with celiac disease and autoimmune thyroid disease, so screening for both starts at diagnosis. Celiac screening is then repeated if symptoms develop, whereas thyroid screening continues annually.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nWaiting 1, 3, 5 or 10 years would miss silent celiac disease, which affects about 5–10% of children with type 1 diabetes and can cause poor growth, hypoglycemia and poor glycemic control. (The later time points are the type of schedule used for microvascular complication screening, not for autoimmune associations.)","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.464 (printed 454) · Answer PDF p.473","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":98,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-100","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other (disease characteristics) · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","q":"Signs of insulin resistance or conditions associated with insulin resistance in T2 DM in children include the following EXCEPT","o":["acanthosis nigricans","hypertension","dyslipidemia","increased waist : hip ratio","polycystic ovary syndrome"],"k":3,"ex":"The source key identifies option D (increased waist : hip ratio) as correct. Review the full explanation for the clinical reasoning.","more":"","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.464 (printed 454) · Answer PDF p.473 (key printed as \"100.(D).\" with no explanation)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":100,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-ENDOCRINEA-104","spec":"endocrinology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis · **Depth:** Moderate","q":"When diabetes and thyroid disease coexist, the possibility of autoimmune adrenal insufficiency should be considered. It may be heralded by the followings ExCEPT","o":["increasing insulin requirements","increasing pigmentation of the skin","salt craving `[label restored as C]`","asthenia","postural hypotension"],"k":0,"ex":"Addison disease is associated with type 1 diabetes and autoimmune thyroid disease. Cortisol deficiency lowers blood glucose, so a patient with diabetes needs less insulin and has more hypoglycemia, not more.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nIncreasing skin pigmentation (high ACTH, especially in palmar creases, scars and mucous membranes), asthenia (fatigue and weakness) and postural hypotension are classic features of Addison disease. Salt craving reflects mineralocorticoid deficiency and sodium loss.\n\nHigh-yield:\nUnexplained hypoglycemia or falling insulin needs in a child with type 1 diabetes should prompt a check for adrenal insufficiency.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.465 (printed 455) · Answer PDF p.474","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Endocrinology","nlNumber":104,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-001","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"When an individual is at-risk genotype and not clinically express the condition; this phenomena is called","o":["genetic imprinting","Bgenetic anticipation","uniparental diasomy","non expressiveness","non penetrance"],"k":4,"ex":"Penetrance is the proportion of people with a disease-causing genotype who show any clinical phenotype. When a person carries the at-risk genotype but shows no features, the gene is non-penetrant (reduced or incomplete penetrance).","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Genetic imprinting: parent-of-origin silencing of a gene (e.g., Prader-Willi/Angelman).\n\n- **B.** Genetic anticipation: earlier onset or worse severity in successive generations (trinucleotide repeat expansion) (OCR: letter spacing lost 'Bgenetic').\n- **C.** Uniparental disomy: both copies of a chromosome inherited from one parent.\n- **D.** 'Non expressiveness': not a standard term; expressivity describes variable severity in those who do show the trait.\n- **E.** Correct (source answer): non-penetrance – genotype present, no phenotype.\n\nNelson explanation:\n1.(E).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source answer key gives the letter only.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 124 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":1,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-002","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"The genetic testing in an asymptomatic child with positive family history of Huntington chorea is called","o":["predisposional testing","predictive testing","diagnostic testing","pharmacogentic testing","linkage testing"],"k":1,"ex":"Predictive (presymptomatic) testing is done on an asymptomatic person with a family history of a late-onset, highly penetrant disorder (e.g., Huntington disease) to find out whether the person will develop it. Diagnostic testing confirms disease in a symptomatic person; predispositional (susceptibility) testing estimates risk for multifactorial disease; pharmacogenetic testing guides drug therapy; linkage testing follows a marker through a family.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Predispositional testing: assesses susceptibility to multifactorial disorders (risk, not certainty).\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer): predictive testing for an asymptomatic at-risk individual (Huntington disease).\n- **C.** Diagnostic testing: confirms a disease in a symptomatic patient.\n- **D.** Pharmacogenetic testing: predicts drug response/toxicity (OCR spelling 'pharmacogentic').\n- **E.** Linkage testing: indirect analysis using markers when the gene or mutation is unknown.\n\nNelson explanation:\n2.(B).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source answer key gives the letter only.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 124 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":2,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-003","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A S-year-old asymptomatic male child with strong positive family history of Huntington chorea, mother is highly concern about her child possible genetic affection, she asked for predictive genetic testing. Of the following, the MOsT appropriate explanation is that","o":["whatever the result it will not affect the disease process","this is an age dependent type of genetic disorder and may not manifest ever","leave the decision to the child when he is grown up","it may lead to discrimination","we should weigh the benefits VS risks of testing"],"k":2,"ex":"Predictive testing of an asymptomatic child for an adult-onset disorder with no childhood treatment is generally deferred until the person can understand the risks and give informed consent. The key accepts that all statements are true, but states the guideline: test children only if the result benefits their medical management; otherwise leave the decision to the child when grown up.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** 'Whatever the result it will not affect the disease process': true, no treatment changes the course.\n\n- **B.** Age-dependent late onset that may never manifest: true (Huntington disease is adult onset with high penetrance).\n- **C.** Correct (source answer): leave the decision to the child when he is grown up (defer until informed consent is possible).\n- **D.** Possible discrimination (insurance, employment, social): true, but not the guideline.\n- **E.** Weigh benefits versus risks: general principle, but the keyed answer is the specific recommendation to defer.\n\nNelson explanation:\n3.(C). In fact, all the mentioned explanations about the disease are true but it is\ngenerally agreed that predictive genetic tests should be performed for children if the\nresults of the test will benefit the medical management of the child. Otherwise, the test\nshould be deferred until the child has an understanding of the risks and benefits of\ntesting and can provide informed consent.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (mild): the key itself says all options are true; option E also states a recognized approach. The keyed answer follows the guideline text. Source key preserved. OCR: age reads 'S-year-old' (probably 5 years).\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 124 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":3,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-004","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"Predispostional genetic testing is more useful in","o":["numerical chromosomal disorders","multifactorial disorders","single gene disorders","structural chromosomal disorders","pre arrangement for gene therapy"],"k":1,"ex":"Predispositional (susceptibility) genetic testing identifies genotypes that increase the risk of multifactorial disease. Knowing the risk lets people change environmental factors or start surveillance (e.g., avoid smoking with alpha-1 antitrypsin variants, early mammography with breast-cancer susceptibility genes).","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Numerical chromosomal disorders: diagnosed by karyotype, not susceptibility testing.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer): multifactorial disorders – risk modification through environment/surveillance.\n- **C.** Single-gene disorders: tested diagnostically/predictively/carrier testing.\n- **D.** Structural chromosomal disorders: karyotype/FISH/microarray.\n- **E.** Pre-arrangement for gene therapy: not the purpose of predispositional testing.\n\nNelson explanation:\n4.(B). In a disease cause by many genes and affected by environmental factors,\ncontrolling of environmental factors may reduce disease progression as in cigarette\nsmoking and a1-antitrypsin deficiency, familial breast cancer and mammography.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 124 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":4,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-005","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"A 4-year-old female child, a known case of ALL, she is developing an increasing toxicity to methotrexate therapy. Of the following, the MOsT useful test is 114 [Continuation on source page 125]","o":["homocysteine level","prothrombin test","factor V leiden","MTHFR DNA testing folate level"],"k":3,"ex":"Pharmacogenetic testing uses an individual's genotype to predict drug response and toxicity. The key cites polymorphisms in the MTHFR gene (methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase) as a marker of increased methotrexate toxicity in acute lymphoblastic leukemia; the testing therefore targets the gene (DNA), not a downstream metabolite.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Homocysteine level: biochemical marker; non-specific and not the genotype test the key requires.\n\n- **B.** Prothrombin test: coagulation/thrombophilia test (OCR: letter B present, text 'prothrombin test').\n- **C.** Factor V Leiden: thrombophilia gene, unrelated to methotrexate toxicity.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): MTHFR DNA testing – pharmacogenetic prediction of methotrexate toxicity.\n- **E.** Folate level: reflects nutrition/supplementation, not genotype (OCR: letter E missing).\n\nNelson explanation:\n5.(D). Knowledge of individual genotypes (pharmacogentic genetic testing) will guide\npharmacologic therapy, allowing customization of choice of drug and dosage to avoid\ntoxicity and provide a therapeutic response. An example of this is testing for\npolymorphisms within the methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) gene for\nsusceptibility of potentially increased toxicity to methotrexate antimetabolite therapy\nfor treatment of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: the stem is split by a page marker ('114', '[Continuation on source page 125]'); options on the next page are intact.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 124 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":5,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-006","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"The following are Indications for genetic counseling ExcEpT","o":["paternal age > 50 years","maternal age > 45 years","family history of intellectual disability","consanguinity","infertility"],"k":1,"ex":"Genetic counseling is indicated for congenital malformations or dysmorphism, developmental delay or intellectual disability, ambiguous genitalia, recurrent miscarriage or infertility, advanced parental age and consanguinity. The key explains that maternal age ≥35 years is an indication and keys 'maternal age > 45 years' as the exception.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Paternal age >50 years: advanced paternal age is an accepted indication (risk of new dominant mutations).\n\n- **B.** Keyed answer (source answer B): 'maternal age >45'. By the key's own rule (≥35 years) this is also an indication, so the item is internally inconsistent.\n- **C.** Family history of intellectual disability: indication.\n- **D.** Consanguinity: indication (higher risk of autosomal recessive disease).\n- **E.** Infertility: indication (chromosomal or genetic causes).\n\nNelson explanation:\n6.(B). Maternal age > or equal to 35 is an indication for genetic counseling. Genetic\ncounseling is a communication process in which the genetic contribution to health is\nexplained, along with specific risks of transmission of a trait and options to manage the\ncondition and its inheritance. The counselor is expected to present information in a\nneutral, nondirective manner and to provide support to the individual and family to\ncope with decisions that are made.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required (significant): all five options are accepted indications, so the keyed exception cannot be justified; recommend moving to NEEDS REVIEW (check whether the original option read '<35' or the stem had a different qualifier). Source answer B preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 125 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":6,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-007","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"The following statements are true about genetic counseling ExCEPT","o":["constructing family pedigree up to the third degree","collecting health files about affected family members","performing appropriate genetic testing","refer to support groups","talking about rewarding gene therapy"],"k":4,"ex":"Genetic counseling is a communication process: take a three-generation family pedigree and health records, arrange appropriate genetic testing, explain recurrence risks and options neutrally (non-directive), and refer to support groups. Gene therapy is still largely experimental, so promising or 'rewarding' gene therapy is not part of routine counseling.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Constructing a pedigree up to the third degree: part of the process.\n\n- **B.** Collecting health records of affected relatives: part of the process.\n- **C.** Performing appropriate genetic testing: part of the process.\n- **D.** Referral to support groups: part of the process.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): discussing rewarding gene therapy – still experimental (key).\n\nNelson explanation:\n7.(E). Gene therapy still experimental and rewarding therapy is still a hope.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 125 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":7,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-009","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"DiGeorge syndrome is an example of","o":["contiguous gene disorders","single gene disorders","mitochondrial inheritance disorders","numeric chromosomal disorders","imprinting disorders"],"k":0,"ex":"Contiguous gene syndromes are caused by deletion (or duplication) of a small chromosomal segment containing several adjacent genes, producing a combined phenotype. DiGeorge/22q11.2 deletion syndrome is the classic example (with Williams 7q11.23, Prader-Willi/Angelman 15q11-q13, Alagille 20p12, Smith-Magenis).","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer): DiGeorge syndrome is a contiguous gene (microdeletion 22q11.2) disorder.\n\n- **B.** Single gene disorders: mutations in one gene (e.g., CF).\n- **C.** Mitochondrial inheritance disorders: mtDNA, maternally inherited.\n- **D.** Numerical chromosomal disorders: aneuploidy (Down, Turner).\n- **E.** Imprinting disorders: Prader-Willi, Angelman, Beckwith-Wiedemann.\n\nNelson explanation:\n9.(A).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source answer key gives the letter only.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 125 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":9,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-013","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"Parent-to-child transmission (vertical transmission) is a character of autosomal dominant inheritance, for many patients with an autosomal dominant disorder there is no history of an affected family member. All the following are true explanations ExCEPT","o":["variable expressiveness","new mutation","somatic mutations (mosaicism)","digenetic inheritance","incomplete penetrance"],"k":3,"ex":"Autosomal dominant disease usually has vertical transmission, but many patients have no affected relative. Reasons: new (de novo) mutation, variable expressivity (a mildly affected parent unrecognized), incomplete penetrance (non-penetrant carrier), and somatic/germline mosaicism.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Variable expressiveness: a parent with a mild form may be missed (true explanation).\n\n- **B.** New mutation: very common in AD disease (e.g., achondroplasia, Marfan).\n- **C.** Somatic mutations (mosaicism): mosaic or gonadal mosaic parents (true explanation).\n- **D.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): digenic inheritance – double heterozygotes for two genes, not an explanation for AD disease (key).\n- **E.** Incomplete penetrance: carrier parent without phenotype (true explanation).\n\nNelson explanation:\n13.(D). Double heterozygotes","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 126 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":13,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-014","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The following are characteristic feature of autosomal dominant inheritance ExcEPT","o":["vertical transmission","male to male transmission","males and females are equally affected","skipped generation","both parents carried the affected gene"],"k":4,"ex":"Autosomal dominant (AD) inheritance: one mutant allele is enough; affected persons usually have an affected parent (vertical transmission), males and females are equally affected, male-to-male transmission occurs, and non-penetrance can make a generation appear to be skipped. The idea that both parents carry the affected gene belongs to autosomal recessive inheritance.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Vertical transmission: characteristic of AD.\n\n- **B.** Male-to-male transmission: occurs in AD (not in X-linked).\n- **C.** Males and females equally affected: characteristic of AD (OCR: letter C lost).\n- **D.** Skipped generation: possible in AD only with non-penetrance, so it is not typical; SHETS lists skipping of generations under autosomal recessive.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): both parents carrying the gene is a feature of autosomal recessive disease (key).\n\nNelson explanation:\n14.(E). Autosomal dominant inheritance is determined by the presence of 1 abnormal\ngene on 1 of the autosomes (chromosomes 1-22). Autosomal genes exist in pairs, with\neach parent contributing 1 copy. In an autosomal dominant trait, a change in 1 of the\npaired genes has an effect on the phenotype.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (mild): option D (skipped generation) is also not classic AD, though possible with reduced penetrance; the clearer exception is E (key).\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 126 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":14,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-015","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are characteristic features of autosomal recessive inheritance EXCEPT","o":["vertical transmission","no affected family members in other generations","males and females are equally affected","consanguinity plays important role","25% recurrence risk"],"k":0,"ex":"Autosomal recessive (AR) disease appears only in homozygotes: both parents are heterozygous carriers (25% recurrence), males and females are equally affected, consanguinity raises the risk, and the pattern is horizontal (siblings affected, no affected relatives in other generations). Vertical transmission is the dominant pattern.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): vertical transmission is a feature of AD, not AR (key says AR is horizontal).\n\n- **B.** No affected members in other generations: typical of AR.\n- **C.** Males and females equally affected: typical of AR.\n- **D.** Consanguinity plays an important role: typical of AR.\n- **E.** 25% recurrence risk per pregnancy: typical of AR.\n\nNelson explanation:\n15.(A). Horizontal transmission is the characteristic feature of autosomal recessive\ninheritance, the observation of multiple affected members of kindred in the same\ngeneration, but no affected family members in other generations.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 126 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":15,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-016","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The risk of a genetic disorder for the offspring of a first-cousin marriage (consanguinity marriage) is","o":["3-5% 116 [Continuation on source page 127]","6-8%","9-11%","12-14%","15-17%"],"k":1,"ex":"The risk of a birth defect or genetic disorder in the offspring of first cousins is about 6–8%, roughly double the general population risk of 3–4%. Cousins share 1/8 of their genes, which increases the chance that a child receives two copies of the same recessive allele.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** 3–5%: close to the general population background risk.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer): 6–8% for first-cousin marriage.\n- **C.** 9–11%: overestimates the risk.\n- **D.** 12–14%: overestimates.\n- **E.** 15–17%: overestimates.\n\nNelson explanation:\n16.(B). The risk of a genetic disorder for the offspring of a first-cousin marriage (6-8%)\nis about double the risk in the general population (3-4%).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: options are split across a page marker ('116', '[Continuation on source page 127]').\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 126 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":16,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-017","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Pseudodominant inheritance happens when","o":["an x-linked disorder appears as autosomal dominant","an autosomal dominant appears as recessive trait","an autosomal recessive disorder appears as autosomal dominant","skipped generations appears","an autosomal dominant appears as x-linked disorder"],"k":2,"ex":"Pseudodominance is the appearance of vertical (parent-to-child) transmission in a known autosomal recessive disease. It occurs when an affected homozygous person has a partner who is a heterozygous carrier (50% of children are affected), most often with common traits such as sickle cell disease.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** X-linked disorder appearing as AD: X-linked dominant, not pseudodominance.\n\n- **B.** AD appearing as recessive: reduced penetrance, not pseudodominance.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer): AR disorder appears as AD.\n- **D.** Skipped generations: horizontal pattern/non-penetrance.\n- **E.** AD appearing as X-linked: not a recognized pattern.\n\nNelson explanation:\n17.(C). Pseudodominant inheritance refers to the observation of apparent dominant\n(parent to child) transmission of a known autosomal recessive disorder. This occurs\nwhen a homozygous affected individual has a partner who is a heterozygous carrier,\nand it is most likely to occur for relatively common traits, such as sickle cell anemia","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 127 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":17,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-018","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"In genetic disorders, male to male transmission occurs in Y- linked inheritance and in","o":["X- linked inheritance","pseudodominant inheritance","digenetic inheritance","autosomal dominant disorder","pseudogenetic inheritance"],"k":3,"ex":"Male-to-male (father-to-son) transmission excludes X-linked inheritance because a father gives his Y chromosome to sons. It occurs in Y-linked inheritance and in autosomal dominant disease (and in autosomal recessive disease with pseudodominance).","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** X-linked inheritance: father passes his X only to daughters; no father-to-son transmission.\n\n- **B.** Pseudodominant inheritance: an AR disease can mimic dominance and an affected father can have an affected son; the key does not choose it.\n- **C.** Digenic inheritance: two genes; not a typical pattern defined by sex of transmitter.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): autosomal dominant disorder.\n- **E.** 'Pseudogenetic inheritance': not a recognized term.\n\nNelson explanation:\n18.(D).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (mild): option B can also show male-to-male transmission; the stem's 'classic' answer is autosomal dominant. Source key preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 127 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":18,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-020","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are features of mitochondrial inheritance ExCEPT","o":["non-traditional inheritance","maternal inheritance","male to offspring transmission","both sexes are affected","mitochondrial DNA point mutations"],"k":2,"ex":"Mitochondrial DNA is inherited from the ovum; sperm mitochondria are lost after fertilization. Therefore the pattern is maternal inheritance: an affected mother transmits to all children (both sexes affected, variable severity because of heteroplasmy), whereas an affected father never transmits it.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Non-traditional (non-Mendelian) inheritance: true.\n\n- **B.** Maternal inheritance: true.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): male-to-offspring transmission does not occur (key).\n- **D.** Both sexes can be affected: true.\n- **E.** mtDNA point mutations: a recognized cause.\n\nNelson explanation:\n20.(C). Mitochondrial inheritance represents a non-traditional inheritance (not follow\nthe usual Mendelian inheritance), both sexes can be affected from the affected\nmitochondrial DNA genome in the ovum (sperm contain few mitochondria which\nfinishes during fertilization), so no male to offspring transmission.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 127 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":20,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-024","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The observation of increasing severity of the disease and early age of onset in subsequent generation is called","o":["genetic imprinting","genetic anticipation","uniparental diasomy","non expressiveness","non penetrance"],"k":1,"ex":"Genetic anticipation is earlier onset and/or increasing severity of a disease in successive generations, typically due to expansion of unstable trinucleotide repeats (e.g., Huntington disease, myotonic dystrophy, fragile X, Friedreich ataxia).","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Genetic imprinting: parent-of-origin gene silencing.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer): genetic anticipation.\n- **C.** Uniparental disomy: both chromosomes from one parent.\n- **D.** Non-expressiveness: not a standard term.\n- **E.** Non-penetrance: carrier without phenotype.\n\nNelson explanation:\n24.(B).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source answer key gives the letter only. The same option list is used in Q1.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 128 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":24,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-026","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The following genetic scenarios are possible to explain Angelman syndrome EXCEPT","o":["inheriting both chromosomes 15 from the father","deletion of the maternal 15q12 deletion of the maternal 15q12","mutation in an imprinted gene deletion of the paternal 15q12","mutation in the imprinting center mutation in an imprinted gene 118 [Continuation on source page 129]","genetic anticipation mutation in the imprinting center"],"k":4,"ex":"Angelman syndrome results from loss of the maternally expressed UBE3A gene in 15q11-q13. Mechanisms: deletion of the maternal 15q11-q13 (~70%), paternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 15 (~3–5%), mutation of UBE3A (an imprinted gene), and imprinting-center defects.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Inheriting both chromosomes 15 from the father (paternal UPD): a recognized mechanism.\n\n- **B.** Deletion of maternal 15q12: the commonest mechanism.\n- **C.** Mutation in an imprinted gene (UBE3A): recognized but less common.\n- **D.** Mutation in the imprinting center: recognized but uncommon.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): genetic anticipation (key).\n\nNelson explanation:\n26.(E). The first statement is called uniparental disomy when both copies of a\nchromosome is inherited from one parent, the second call genetic imprinting when one\n124\n[Source page 135]\ngene copy is silenced in one side; either in mother or father resulting in each situation\nin different syndromes, third and fourth distracters is possible but are uncommon\ncauses.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR contamination: the block contains a second, different option list after the stem ('B. deletion of the maternal 15q12 / C. deletion of the paternal 15q12 / D. mutation in an imprinted gene / E. mutation in the imprinting center'), with option E different from the first list. The analysis above follows the first list (E = genetic anticipation), which matches the key. Verify against PDF pages 128–129.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 128 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":26,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-028","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The recurrence risk of multifactorial inheritance are characterized by all the following EXCEPT","o":["related to the incidence of the disease in community","similar among all 1st-degree relatives","no sex predilection","increased when multiple family members are affected","greater when the disorder is more severe"],"k":2,"ex":"Recurrence risk in multifactorial disease depends on the incidence in the population, is higher when several relatives are affected, is higher when the disorder is more severe, and is highest for first-degree relatives. Many multifactorial disorders have a sex predilection (pyloric stenosis in boys; developmental dysplasia of the hip in girls), and the risk is higher for relatives of an index case of the less commonly affected sex.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Related to the incidence in the community: true.\n\n- **B.** Similar among all first-degree relatives: true per the source (risk is about the same for siblings, parents and children).\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): many multifactorial disorders do have a sex predilection (pyloric stenosis, DDH).\n- **D.** Increased when multiple family members are affected: true.\n- **E.** Greater when the disorder is more severe: true.\n\nNelson explanation:\n28.(C). Some multifactorial inherited disorders have a sex predilection, as indicated by\nan unequal male: female incidence. Pyloric stenosis, for example, is more common in\nmales, whereas congenital dislocation of the hips is more common in females. Where\nthere is an altered sex ratio, the risk is higher for the relatives of an index case whose\ngender is less commonly affected than relatives of an index case of the more commonly\naffected gender. For example, the risk to the son of an affected female with infantile\npyloric stenosis is 18%, compared with the 5% risk for the son of an affected male. An\naffected female presumably has a greater genetic susceptibility, which she can then\npass on to her offspring.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 129 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":28,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-029","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Chromosomal analysis is indicated in all the following EXCEPT","o":["recurrent premature deliveries","postnatal problems in growth and development","unexplained intellectual disability","primary amenorrhea","recurrent miscarriages (≥3)"],"k":0,"ex":"Chromosomal analysis (karyotype/microarray) is indicated for multiple congenital anomalies, ambiguous genitalia, unexplained intellectual disability or developmental delay, growth failure, primary amenorrhea or infertility, recurrent miscarriage (≥3), stillbirth/neonatal death, clinically recognizable chromosomal syndromes, certain malignancies, and breakage syndromes. Recurrent premature delivery is not a chromosomal indication.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): recurrent premature deliveries are not an indication (key).\n\n- **B.** Postnatal problems in growth and development: indication.\n- **C.** Unexplained intellectual disability: indication.\n- **D.** Primary amenorrhea: indication (e.g., Turner).\n- **E.** Recurrent miscarriages (≥3): indication (parental balanced translocation).\n\nNelson explanation:\n29.(A). The specific indications for studies include advanced maternal age (>35 yr) or\nmultiple abnormalities on fetal ultrasound (prenatal testing), multiple congenital\nanomalies, unexplained growth restriction in the fetus or postnatal problems in growth\nand development, ambiguous genitalia, unexplained intellectual disability with or\nwithout associated anatomic abnormalities, primary amenorrhea or infertility,\nrecurrent miscarriages (≥3) or prior history of stillbirths and neonatal deaths, a 1st-\ndegree relative with a known or suspected structural chromosome abnormality, clinical\nfindings consistent with a known anomaly, some malignancies, and chromosome\nbreakage syndromes (e.g., Bloom syndrome, Fanconi anemia).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 129 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":29,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-032","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are recognized gastrointestinal features of Down syndrome in neonatal period EXCEPT","o":["annular pancreas","tracheoesophageal fistula","diaphragmatic hernia","imperforate anus","neonatal cholestasis"],"k":2,"ex":"Down syndrome has a high rate of gastrointestinal anomalies in the neonatal period: duodenal atresia/stenosis and annular pancreas, tracheoesophageal fistula, imperforate anus, Hirschsprung disease and neonatal cholestasis; later celiac disease and GERD. Diaphragmatic hernia is not a recognized feature.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Annular pancreas: recognized (with duodenal atresia).\n\n- **B.** Tracheoesophageal fistula: recognized.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): diaphragmatic hernia is not typical for Down syndrome (key).\n- **D.** Imperforate anus: recognized.\n- **E.** Neonatal cholestasis: recognized.\n\nNelson explanation:\n32.(C). It also includes duodenal atresia and Hirschsprung disease.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 129 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":32,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-033","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"The MOsT important reason for chromosomal analysis in persons suspected of having Down syndrome is to 119 [Continuation on source page 130]","o":["confirm clinical diagnosis","detect mosaicism","reassure the family","detect translocations","detect rare cases of ring chromosome"],"k":3,"ex":"Chromosome analysis is indicated in every child with suspected Down syndrome because the karyotype distinguishes standard trisomy 21 (~94%, low recurrence), mosaicism (~1–2%) and translocation (~4–5%). The most important reason is to detect a translocation, because a parent may be a balanced carrier with a high recurrence risk, so parental karyotyping is required.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Confirm the clinical diagnosis: a reason, but not the most important.\n\n- **B.** Detect mosaicism: affects phenotype and prognosis, but does not change recurrence risk as much.\n- **C.** Reassure the family: not a purpose.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): detect translocations – recurrence risk and parental testing.\n- **E.** Detect rare cases of ring chromosome: very rare.\n\nNelson explanation:\n33.(D). Chromosome analysis is indicated in every person suspected of having Down\nsyndrome. If a translocation is identified, parental chromosome studies must be\nperformed to determine whether one of the parents is a translocation carrier, which\ncarries a high recurrence risk for having another affected child.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: the stem is split by a page marker ('119', '[Continuation on source page 130]').\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 129 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":33,"f":true}]);